body=['<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Findings on your 27 boundary notes</title><style>body{font:16px system-ui;max-width:1250px;margin:35px;color:#263744;line-height:1.5}section{border-top:1px solid #bbb;margin-top:30px;padding-top:15px}blockquote{background:#f3f4f6;padding:12px}svg{width:100%;height:auto}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:12px}details{margin:12px 0}</style><h1>Investigation of your 27 boundary-review notes</h1><p>Read-only investigation of current published geometry, selected-parent contracts, original reference frames, October 3 pre-repair hydraulic snapshots, and October 8 manual/automated repair records. Your original decisions are preserved in user_review.json. No production inputs or existing review files changed.</p><p><b>Viewer correction:</b> the old viewer labelled the complement of successfully matched exterior paths as replacement. A failed exterior match is not evidence of membership. It also omitted labels for several new interior/degree-two nodes and displayed automated boundaries alongside manually waived boundaries. The maps below label all visible nodes; orange means investigated, never selected. Only reference teal is the saved selected region.</p><p><b>What is established:</b> the repeated loops and added nodes predate the boundary repairs where checked. The exact raster-generation operation that created them has not been reproduced. Existing source comments explain why coincident nodes were assigned distinct hydraulic identities; that identity policy does not justify changing the exterior geometry.</p><p><b>Slope consequence:</b> recognise legitimate exterior chains before defining the free-head region. Small length changes still matter: fixed endpoint drops and fixed individual slopes cannot both be preserved when lengths change. Exterior reversals need explicit head flexibility or topology changes. Previous manual merges/waivers must be represented as explicit scope exceptions.</p><nav>']
